WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:02.843
This is a tensile testing machine.

2
00:00:03.595 --> 00:00:07.879
At the bottom is a fixed grip, at the top a crosshead that slowly moves up.

3
00:00:08.618 --> 00:00:11.381
Between them we clamp a round steel test bar.

4
00:00:12.386 --> 00:00:15.718
In a tensile test you pull the bar until it breaks.

5
00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:18.140
A load cell measures the force,

6
00:00:18.372 --> 00:00:22.192
and an extensometer on the bar measures how much longer it gets.

7
00:00:22.885 --> 00:00:26.438
From that the computer draws the line of stress against strain.

8
00:00:27.437 --> 00:00:31.977
Stress is the force divided by the bar's cross-section, in megapascals.

9
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.219
Strain is how much longer the bar gets, divided by its original length.

10
00:00:37.977 --> 00:00:40.624
That way thick and thin bars compare fairly.

11
00:00:41.627 --> 00:00:45.238
At first the line runs straight up: the elastic range.

12
00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:47.589
The bar behaves like a spring.

13
00:00:48.311 --> 00:00:51.899
Take the force away and it springs back to its old length.

14
00:00:52.624 --> 00:00:55.910
How steep that line runs is the modulus of elasticity.

15
00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.201
The card stretches this part:

16
00:00:58.352 --> 00:01:02.113
real steel strains only one or two tenths of a percent here.

17
00:01:03.464 --> 00:01:05.020
Then the line bends over.

18
00:01:05.791 --> 00:01:10.354
That point is the yield strength: there the steel starts to deform permanently.

19
00:01:11.046 --> 00:01:13.263
The metal starts to flow, as it were.

20
00:01:14.279 --> 00:01:18.957
Take the force away now, and the line drops back parallel to the straight part,

21
00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:20.897
leaving some strain behind.

22
00:01:21.649 --> 00:01:23.054
The bar has stayed longer.

23
00:01:24.085 --> 00:01:28.682
European structural steels carry their minimum yield strength in their name.

24
00:01:29.359 --> 00:01:38.705
S235 yields at 235 megapascals or more, S355 at 355.

25
00:01:39.676 --> 00:01:44.471
That holds for plate up to 16 mm thick; thicker plate is slightly lower.

26
00:01:45.195 --> 00:01:47.935
Designers keep the stress below the yield strength,

27
00:01:48.132 --> 00:01:52.439
with a margin: a part that bends permanently has usually already failed.

28
00:01:53.816 --> 00:01:56.184
Pull on and the stress rises further still.

29
00:01:56.899 --> 00:02:00.672
Over the whole gauge length the bar gets evenly thinner and longer,

30
00:02:00.846 --> 00:02:04.085
and the steel even gets a little stronger as it does.

31
00:02:05.084 --> 00:02:08.114
The highest point of the line is the tensile strength,

32
00:02:08.335 --> 00:02:11.481
or UTS: the greatest stress the material reaches.

33
00:02:12.187 --> 00:02:16.123
You work it out from the highest force and the original cross-section.

34
00:02:17.189 --> 00:02:18.292
Then the bar necks.

35
00:02:19.022 --> 00:02:23.851
In one spot it gets visibly thinner, all further stretching goes into that neck,

36
00:02:24.037 --> 00:02:26.568
and the force needed to keep pulling drops.

37
00:02:27.561 --> 00:02:29.465
Finally it breaks in the neck.

38
00:02:30.176 --> 00:02:33.787
But for a design the limit is the yield strength, not the break.

39
00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:36.979
Bolts carry a property class.

40
00:02:37.673 --> 00:02:40.680
The head shows, for example, eight point eight.

41
00:02:41.788 --> 00:02:46.722
The first number times one hundred is the tensile strength in megapascals.

42
00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:50.727
Eight times one hundred: 800 megapascals.

43
00:02:51.735 --> 00:02:54.695
The second number times ten is the yield strength,

44
00:02:54.881 --> 00:02:57.273
as a percentage of the tensile strength.

45
00:02:57.985 --> 00:03:02.130
80 percent of 800 is 640 megapascals.

46
00:03:03.146 --> 00:03:05.816
Those are the nominal values you calculate with.

47
00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:10.667
So never replace a broken bolt with whatever happens to be in the drawer:

48
00:03:10.818 --> 00:03:12.548
a lower class yields sooner.

49
00:03:13.866 --> 00:03:18.452
The steel we just pulled is ductile: it stretches a lot before it breaks.

50
00:03:19.212 --> 00:03:23.427
That gives a warning, because a part bends, necks or sags first.

51
00:03:24.197 --> 00:03:27.587
Most steels, aluminium and copper are ductile.

52
00:03:28.592 --> 00:03:30.519
Now a bar of grey cast iron.

53
00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:35.841
It is brittle: the line stops almost at once, and it breaks without necking.

54
00:03:36.562 --> 00:03:40.915
Glass, ceramics and very hard steel break the same way, without warning.

55
00:03:41.922 --> 00:03:43.698
Cold makes some steels brittle.

56
00:03:44.405 --> 00:03:49.142
The impact test measures that: a notched bar is broken by a swinging hammer.

57
00:03:49.846 --> 00:03:54.490
The less high the hammer swings on afterwards, the more energy the bar absorbed.

58
00:03:55.557 --> 00:03:58.924
A common requirement, for example for grade J2,

59
00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:02.756
is at least 27 joules at minus 20 degrees.

60
00:04:03.473 --> 00:04:08.140
Plenty of energy in the cold means this steel stays ductile in winter too.

61
00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.653
That value is on the batch's material certificate,

62
00:04:11.838 --> 00:04:15.356
often a three point one certificate, next to the measured yield,

63
00:04:15.472 --> 00:04:18.212
tensile strength and elongation at break.

64
00:04:18.924 --> 00:04:21.443
Ask for it on safety-critical parts.

65
00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:24.222
How much margin do you have?

66
00:04:24.927 --> 00:04:29.014
The safety factor is the yield strength divided by the working stress,

67
00:04:29.211 --> 00:04:30.871
the stress in normal use.

68
00:04:31.609 --> 00:04:37.901
A bracket of S235 sees a working stress of 120 megapascals:

69
00:04:38.041 --> 00:04:43.509
235 divided by 120 is 1.96.

70
00:04:44.219 --> 00:04:45.647
Fine for a steady load.

71
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.457
After a process change the load doubles: 240 megapascals.

72
00:04:52.172 --> 00:04:56.131
That is above the yield strength, so the bracket will bend permanently.

73
00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:05.429
Switching to S355 gives 355 divided by 240, that is 1.48.

74
00:05:06.183 --> 00:05:09.724
It holds, but the margin is thin if the load vibrates,

75
00:05:09.898 --> 00:05:12.174
and then fatigue may be the next problem.

76
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:18.659
A thicker bracket in S235 brings the stress back to 120,

77
00:05:18.832 --> 00:05:20.620
and restores the old margin.

78
00:05:21.589 --> 00:05:24.201
Typical is about 1.5 for well-known,

79
00:05:24.329 --> 00:05:28.230
steady loads, and 3 or more for uncertain or dynamic ones.

80
00:05:28.939 --> 00:05:33.247
Lifting gear uses 4 to 7 against breaking, depending on the type.

81
00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:38.027
The factor covers the unknowns; it is not room for extra load.

82
00:05:38.763 --> 00:05:42.385
A factor of two against breaking is not two against yield,

83
00:05:42.536 --> 00:05:46.623
and a factor on the static weight says little about a load that vibrates.

84
00:05:47.309 --> 00:05:51.118
So always ask: safety factor against what, and on which load?
